    "result_text": "The build passes. **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\nThe audit log confirms the runtime behavior end-to-end:\n- `memberId=V2NSW2616E6609E9` \u2014 the V2-tagged region code now lives inside the identifier (was `NSW26\u2026` under v1).\n- `{\"seq\":1,\"schemaVersion\":2,\u2026,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}` \u2014 audit event moved to schema version 2.\n\n## What changed and why\n\n**Version-2 identity derivation** \u2014 a single `VERSION_TAG = \"V2\"` constant lives on `CustomerCode`; the other derivations reference it, so the tag is defined once.\n- `CustomerCode.region(owner)` now returns `\"V2\" + region` (e.g. `V2NSW`), so the memberId's region prefix \u2014 and therefore the whole memberId and its Luhn digit \u2014 changes. `plainRegion(...)` strips the tag back to `NSW` for user-facing use.\n- `Household.idFor` and `IdentityKey.of` mix `\"V2|\"` into their hashed inputs, so both digests differ from every v1 value.\n- `MemberId` was left untouched \u2014 it re-uses `CustomerCode`, so the new region flows through for free.\n\n**Response shape** (`openapi.yml` + `OwnerMapper`)\n- Added top-level `apiVersion: 2` and a nested `Identity` schema (`memberId`, `identityKey`, `householdId`); removed those three from the top level. The mapper sets `apiVersion` via a constant and builds `identity` in a small `@AfterMapping` method.\n\n**User-facing fields stay plain** \u2014 `locality` and `timezone` mappings are unchanged (`NSW` / `Australia/Sydney`). The `ownerSegment` is recomputed from the v2 identity's region (`CustomerCode.region(owner)`) with the tag stripped via `plainRegion`, so its derived region stays plain (`STANDARD_METRO` unchanged) \u2014 `OwnerSegment` itself didn't need editing.\n\n**Audit** \u2014 `OwnerCreatedEvent` emits `\"schemaVersion\":2` (one-line format change), sourcing the owner segment/identity from the v2 memberId already on the owner.\n\n## Structural impact\nSix files touched, each edit small and local: `MemberId` and `OwnerSegment` (the two classes most entangled with this logic) were left unchanged, no method grew in complexity, and no new file was added \u2014 the shared tag rides on the existing `CustomerCode` class rather than a new one.",
